// Reading a leaf's arguments.
//
// `i` is the argument's position, and position decides kind -- idml's own
// convention (research/idml-reference.md §4.1), which is why `Text("hi", 0xC8C8C8)`
// needs no keywords. An absent argument answers the supplied default, so every
// leaf has a sensible form with no arguments at all.

ui_arg(int nd, int i, int v) {
  int a = ast_ia(nd);
  if (a != 0) {
    int[] ast_ks_v = ast_ks(a);
    if (i < len(ast_ks_v)) {
      v = ast_ia(ast_ks_v[i]);
    }
  }
} return int v;
given ast_init (0, 0, 77):(77)
given ui_item_fx2 (7, 0, 99):(200)
given ui_item_fx2 (7, 2, 99):(99)

ui_sarg(int nd, int i, string s) {
  int a = ast_ia(nd);
  if (a != 0) {
    int[] ast_ks_v = ast_ks(a);
    if (i < len(ast_ks_v)) {
      s = ast_tx(ast_ks_v[i]);
    }
  }
} return string s;
given ast_init (0, 0, "def"):("def")
given ui_item_fx2 (7, 1, "def"):("hi")
given ui_item_fx2 (7, 2, "def"):("def")

// Text is anchored inside its rect by the anchor word's horizontal half, which is
// the one part of an anchor a single line of text can honour. `left` is the
// default because an unrecognised anchor falls back to the start of both axes.
// Drawn at the UI's own scale, not the window's. A game's interface belongs to
// its *stage* -- the rect its declared units map onto -- so a label is exactly as
// big relative to the game as the sprite beside it, at every window size and at
// every zoom of the editor's panel. Asking txt_ui here instead is what made text
// in a zoomed panel jump between whole steps while everything around it moved
// smoothly, and what made a packed game's HUD shrink as its window grew.
// A literal string, or a `@ref` drawn as its value. `Text("SCORE")` and
// `Text(@score)` are the same item with a different first argument, which is what
// lets a screen be laid out once and filled from the world every frame.
ui_text(int nd, int x, int y, int w, int h) {
  int ui_arg0_v = ui_arg0(nd);
  if (ast_kd(ui_arg0_v) = (import ast_k_ref)) {
    ui_tnum(nd, x, y, w, h);
  } else {
    ui_tstr(nd, x, y, w, h);
  }
} return void;
given txt_init given sf_t_setup given ui_item_fx2 (7, 0, 0, 100, 20):() then calls ui_tstr:[1]
